

[38;5;231m⏺[39m That's all the decisions. Here's the design in three sections; I'll check with you after each one.

  [1mSection 1: Components[0m

  [1mNew [38;5;153msession.js[0m (ES module at the repo root, next to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m). It's the only code that touches the stored user:

  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mcreateSession[39m(storage = globalThis.localStorage) → {
    [33msetCurrentUser[39m({ userId, username }),  [32m// stamps loggedInAt, writes JSON[39m
    [33mgetCurrentUser[39m(),                      [32m// → { userId, username, loggedInAt } | null[39m
    [33mclearCurrentUser[39m(),
  }
  [34mexport[39m [34mconst[39m session = [33mcreateSession[39m();  [32m// default instance forms import[39m

  - Storage key: [38;5;153m"currentUser"[39m, holding a JSON record [38;5;153m{ userId, username, loggedInAt }[39m where [38;5;153mloggedInAt[39m is an ISO
    timestamp.
  - [38;5;153mcreateSession(storage)[39m takes the storage as an argument so tests can pass in a fake, and so a server-backed version
    can replace it later without changing any form.

  [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[39m changes:[0m
  - The signature stays [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m. It now returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m. The stub sets [38;5;153muserId = [39m
    [38;5;153musername[39m and marks the spot where the real API response's ID goes.
  - After a successful login, the submit handler calls [38;5;153msession.setCurrentUser(...)[39m. Writing the session is the caller's
    job, not [38;5;153mlogin()[39m's. That keeps [38;5;153mlogin()[39m a pure "verify credentials" call.
  - It gains [38;5;153mimport { session } from './session.js'[39m.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m:[0m [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m.

  [1mNot included (YAGNI):[0m a logout button or UI. [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser()[39m exists for when one is added. Also not included: login
  history and any server-side session.

  Does this section look right?

